    <name>
      <text>Shared: Complete tables from different linear representations</text>
    </name>


    <questiontext format="html">
      <text>&lt;p&gt;
Two linear relations are given in
different forms.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The first relation is
\[
y={@m1@}x+({@c1@}).
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The second relation is
\[
({@a2@})x+z={@b2@}.
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Complete the missing values in both
rows of the table.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
For the second relation, you may find
it helpful to rearrange the equation
so that \(z\) is by itself.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;table border="1"
       cellpadding="8"
       style="border-collapse:collapse;
              text-align:center;"&gt;

&lt;tr&gt;
  &lt;th&gt;\(x\)&lt;/th&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;\({@x1@}\)&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;\({@x2@}\)&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;\({@x3@}\)&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;td&gt;\({@x4@}\)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;

&lt;tr&gt;
  &lt;th&gt;\(y\)&lt;/th&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    \({@y1@}\)
  &lt;/td&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    [[input:ans1]]
    [[validation:ans1]]
    [[feedback:prt1]]
  &lt;/td&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    [[input:ans2]]
    [[validation:ans2]]
    [[feedback:prt2]]
  &lt;/td&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    \({@y4@}\)
  &lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;

&lt;tr&gt;
  &lt;th&gt;\(z\)&lt;/th&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    [[input:ans3]]
    [[validation:ans3]]
    [[feedback:prt3]]
  &lt;/td&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    \({@z2@}\)
  &lt;/td&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    \({@z3@}\)
  &lt;/td&gt;

  &lt;td&gt;
    [[input:ans4]]
    [[validation:ans4]]
    [[feedback:prt4]]
  &lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;

&lt;/table&gt;
      </text>
    </questiontext>


    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text>&lt;h3&gt;Worked solution&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
For the first relation,
\[
y={@m1@}x+({@c1@}).
\]
Substitute each \(x\)-value directly.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
For example, at \(x={@x2@}\),
\[
y=({@m1@})({@x2@})+({@c1@})
={@y2@}.
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The second relation is
\[
({@a2@})x+z={@b2@}.
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Rearrange it:
\[
z={@b2@}-({@a2@})x.
\]
This is equivalent to
\[
z={@m2@}x+({@c2@}).
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
At \(x={@x1@}\),
\[
z={@b2@}-({@a2@})({@x1@})
={@z1@}.
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
At \(x={@x4@}\),
\[
z={@b2@}-({@a2@})({@x4@})
={@z4@}.
\]
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
The important step is recognizing that
the second relation represents a
straight-line rule even though it was
not initially written with \(z\)
isolated.
&lt;/p&gt;
      </text>
    </generalfeedback>


